    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Detailed mapping of outcrops of the late Miocene Sisquoc Formation at Jalama Beach, central California, shows relationships between faults, zones of brecciation, faulting-related fractures, and hydrocarbon transport. In general, hydrocarbons are confined to areas of brecciated host rock and connected fractures, while fractures further from the shear zones do not contain hydrocarbons. In this paper and an accompanying poster, we document these relationships and conclude that faults with breccia zones and associated fractures are major pathways for fluid transport in these units.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Brankman, Charles M. and Roznovsky, Thomas. (1996). Hydrocarbon Transport in Brecciated Fault Zones: Examples From the Sisquoc Formation, Coastal California. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: http://purl.stanford.edu/kv814yt7013",
            "type": "preferred citation",
